|  Process A PCB |                 |  Process B PCB |
|----------------|                 |----------------|
| Registers      |                 | Registers      |
| Program Counter|                 | Program Counter|
| Stack Pointer  |                 | Stack Pointer  |
+----------------+                 +----------------+
        ^                                  ^
        |                                  |
        |                                  |
        |                                  |
+----------------+                 +----------------+
|  CPU Registers |                 |  CPU Registers |
| (Current State)|                 | (Loaded State) |
+----------------+                 +----------------+
        |                                  |
        |                                  |
        |                                  |
        +------------ Context Switch ------+
Key Components Saved During Context Switch
Program Counter (PC): Points to the next instruction to execute.
CPU Registers: General purpose registers, status registers.
Stack Pointer (SP): Points to the current stack frame.
Memory Management Info: Page tables or segment registers.
Process State: Ready, Running, Waiting, etc.

When a process is created in Unix:

The parent process calls fork().
The kernel creates a new PCB and duplicates the parent's memory space.
The child process receives a unique PID.
The child can call exec() to load a new program.
The scheduler manages execution of the new process.
This fork-exec model is the cornerstone of Unix process creation, enabling flexible and efficient multitasking.
